<TopicPage slug="subjects/biomedical-engineering/foundational/physiology-for-engineers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Biomedical engineering starts by reading the body as an engineered system: pumps, pipes,
    filters, sensors and control loops, running wet. The payoff is enormous — once you see the
    cardiovascular system as fluid mechanics and the kidney as a mass-transfer device, you can
    design devices that intervene in them. The prerequisite is learning the physiology honestly.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the cardiovascular system as a circuit" widget="equationStep" config={cvCfg} />

  <h2>The body obeys the engineers' laws</h2>
  <p>
    Blood is a fluid: it obeys continuity and Poiseuille's law, which is why arterioles — the
    narrowest control points — are where the body regulates flow. Lungs are mass exchangers: ~70
    square metres of alveolar membrane moving oxygen in and carbon dioxide out across a barrier
    half a micrometre thick. Kidneys filter 180 litres a day and reabsorb all but 1.5 litres. The
    numbers are engineering numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>Control everywhere</h2>
  <p>
    Physiology is thick with feedback: baroreflexes holding pressure, insulin and glucagon holding
    glucose, thermoregulation holding 37 °C. The vocabulary is control theory — sensors, set
    points, actuators, disturbance rejection — and diseases often read as controller failures:
    diabetes as a broken glucose loop, hypertension as a reset pressure set point.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this matters for devices</h2>
  <p>
    Every successful medical device respects the physiology it touches. Pacemakers speak the heart's
    electrical dialect; dialysers match the kidney's selectivity; ventilators negotiate lung
    compliance. Get the engineering right and the physiology wrong, and the device fails — which is
    why this course is the first one BME students take, not the last.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET biomedical program criteria; OpenStax <em>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</em>; Boron &amp; Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
